  "blocksOrielShowed": [
    "Computed by Oriel from the index and its change log: Counted by State, highest first",
    "Computed by Oriel from the index and its change log: Done, counted by week, running, since Sep 2. Least-squares line over 7 whole weeks, R² 0.94, 0.8 a week. At that rate the 9 left get there around Jan 14 (Dec 21 – Mar 16, 80%), if no more are added",
    "Computed by Oriel from the index and its change log: Item, State, Assignee, Estimate, Days in its state. ≥ marks a value that is only a lower bound: it began before the change log starts"
  ],
  "reply": "No. At the pace the team has kept so far, SYN-1 Checkout v2: phone wallets and saved cards won't be finished by the end of October.\n\nThis workstream has no items yet, so I searched outside it to find the epic. Here's where it stands as of 29 October:\n\n- **5 of its 14 items are done.** 9 are still open: 4 in progress and 5 not started, with only two days left in the month.\n- **About 0.8 items get finished a week**, steady since early September. The chart below has Oriel's forecast for when the open items would be done, if nothing new is added.\n- **Some items haven't moved in a long time.** SYN-88 Refund to the original payment method when a sailing is cancelled, SYN-98 Apply promo codes at checkout and SYN-99 Save a card for next time haven't been started in about 8 weeks. SYN-57 Checkout analytics: drop-off by step and SYN-10 Remove the legacy web-view checkout haven't been started in 7 to 8 weeks. SYN-21 Update the payment declined error message has been in progress for 3 weeks.\n- **Three open items have no estimate:** SYN-23 Pay with a phone wallet on the website, SYN-99 Save a card for next time and SYN-104 Handle 3-D Secure challenges in the native checkout. That means the remaining work could be bigger than it looks.\n\nIf you want to keep track of this epic, I can make a workstream from its items."
}

Claims:
1. The reply gives no finish date, rate or forecast of its own: any it gives is the one Oriel's block caption states, or it says Oriel had too little history to forecast. A yes or no to the question that follows from Oriel's forecast is not one of its own.
